nd what that means  okay  cool it's like good at least half of the  audience I'd say so I struggled to  define this for a very long time until  this guy Jason Bailey very recently  published an article about it  he he does a lot of work with like art  history and he published this really  good article about the history of  generative art but his definition as  noted in the article is generative art  is art programmed using a computer that  intentionally introduces randomness as a  part of its creation process and I think  that's very precise people have  different differing definitions about  this but I think this one's really good  so if you kind of remove the computer  component of this you might have  splatter art or something like that art  that kind of has a little bit of  randomness involved with it kind of like  Jackson Pollock's paintings and if you  remove the randomness then you get  digital art so traditional kind of  digital paintings or what have you but  when you combine the two you get  generative art and I've found that my  process is a lot of trying to balance  the ideas of kind of complete control  and complete chaos to produce something  that is like visually appealing but not  boring and not too not too crazy and I  call that the sweet spot the interesting  thing is that computers are really good  at both they're really good at  generating completely random garbage  data and they're really good at doing  things completely perfectly so you  really have to a lot of my work is just  trying to balance those concepts  together to produce something visually  appealing  so as programmers we the first step  towards doing anything is gathering  domain knowledge about that thing so I  feel like I should talk a little bit  about the domain of art and and what it  is so this is kind of what you might  learn in a in a high school college  course on  art you know 101 the elements and  principles of artwork so this is a piece  by PM andre on composition 2 in red blue  and yellow and it displays a bunch of  these elements of art which I'll go  through now so you've got color  obvious form is kind of 3d form or the  implication of 3d form which isn't here  and that's okay  lines shapes space refers to like  negative and positive space so for  example in this piece you've got  positive space in the red blue and  yellow portions and then those white  pieces kind of fall into the background  that's called negative space texture  again there's not a lot of texture here  but that's okay  and value refers to the brightness of  colors in your in your artwork and we'll  talk a little bit more about that later  here as well and then I like to add an  eighth element of generative art because  you can't really have generative art  without randomness as we just said but  randomness is another kind of component  just a primary component of of artwork  and the elements of artwork are just the  things that you use to create art and  then the principles of art are kind of  how you use those elements to produce  something that means something and so  these are things like balance which  refers to symmetry and asymmetry  emphasis which refers to you know  focusing on a certain portion of the  artwork as opposed to something else  things that might fall into the  background movement is kind of how your  eye moves through the artwork proportion  thing things can be bigger than others  rhythm is this idea that you might have  some repetition in your artwork but it  may not be perfect and so you have to  kind of carefully consider how that  factors into your compositions and then  unity and variety refer to kind of the  sameness or difference in your work so I  might have squares and circles in my  artwork and you know the squares might  be clustered together that's unity  but you also have some circles there and  that's variety and I additionally I like  to add an eighth principle of generative  art is what I call the eighth principle  of generative art emergence very very  common in generative art is this idea  that the the piece of art just kind of  blossoms into something that you didn't  expect and that's this idea of emergence  and you can see it pretty clearly in a  lot of generative artwork so on the  right we've got a piece by Kandinsky  composition 8 and you can kind of dig in  and see some of these principles in  action one of my favorite is the  movement of your eye when you look  through this piece is really interesting  there are so many lines kind of pointing  in different directions but your eye  just kind of follows them throughout the  whole piece and you can kind of pick up  on some other things as you as your eye  moves through it  so elements I like to think of as being  provided by a tool so a pen will give  you a line maybe a sponge with with  paint on it will give you like a texture  and so those kind of those things are  are kind of provided to you by a tool  which in our case might be a graphics  library like processing or I personally  use Cairo and the principles of art are  those elements of your compositions that  the human puts there so things like  symmetry you're gonna have to explicitly  state you're gonna have to say I want  cemetry in my piece if you want a sense  of rhythm in your piece you're gonna  have to carefully craft that somehow and  so we use the the elements are kind of  given to us and the principles are what  we what we can what we want to achieve  using those elements cool so onto  generative art what is the generative  arts canvas I would say  is for me at least is the the two  dimensional Cartesian plane so just as a  visual artist that works in 2d might  work on a canvas we work on the  Cartesian plane typically this is  represented by computers as zero zeros  in the top left I don't know why there  might be some historic reason for that  but uh it's a common like gotcha at the  beginning and kind of messing around  this sort of thing something to look out  for so we can represent points on our on  our canvas as 2d card Cartesian  coordinates and we can modify those  things using vectors and linear algebra  I think it's really it's not hard to get  a basic understanding of linear algebra  and it's super helpful in kind of  getting your art to do things that you  might want it to do so for example if  you have two points on the Cartesian  plane you can translate one by the other  and that's a linear algebra vector  operation and linear algebra gives us  the ability to implement like scaling of  shapes translation of shapes rotations  of things and all that good stuff so  it's it's really useful to have that in  our tool belt cool and so the next thing  that I'll talk about we just talked  about kind of single points the next  thing that we might want to do is  connect two points together lines line  segments are really great at doing this  so you might have a class in your in  your program that just stores two  different Cartesian coordinates and you  can draw a line between them and this is  a really really good primitive thing to  have in your program because it you can  combine them in really interesting ways  and sort of the generalization of that  is the path so a path is endpoints all  connected in a single line so you can  see on the animation I'm just generating  a whole bunch of points in the middle  there and then  connecting them all with a path and you  can see it just kind of goes most  graphics libraries really provide you  this nicety out of the box so you can  just say like start path draw some  points and path done and then very  similarly for shapes so you can see in  this we're doing the same kind of thing  except generating points kind of around  a little rectangle and then connecting  those points at the end and filling in  the shape I you can also most graphics  libraries will give you the option of  drawing like a rectangle or a circle and  those are really common things to do as  well but you can also do kind of  freeform shapes like this which is a  really nice tool cool and so now I want  to talk a little bit about the element  of randomness in generative art these  are two of my pieces wired T and yr s  they were generated from the same  program on you there are three kind of  key pieces of this that vary a bunch the  first is the kind of obvious one of the  wires don't follow the same path so they  they kind of follow different paths the  second thing that you might notice is  that the fraying on the wires is totally  different so why are s has this kind of  interesting bend in one of the wires  that I really like  whereas YRT doesn't really have that but  they both have that fraying property and  then the third thing that I like to  point out that's extremely subtle is  that the the stippling on the back in  these little rectangles in the  background while they have the same  value kind of going down they they  follow the same kind of value  progression the actual distributions of  those dots is totally different so if  you look super closely then you'll see  that the dots are not the same so how do  we do this we use pseudo random number  generation typically your whatever  programming language you're using is  going to have some way of generating a  random value I use live  that gives me a little bit more than  that so I can get generate normally  distributed or Gaussian distributed  values using my tools and there are  implementations out there for a lot of  things so I wanted to kind of walk  through what each of these things is  doing I'm filling in for squares with  random dots in each in each square but  they look totally different because the  way that we're generating those points  is totally different  so at the top right is kind of the most  basic one  it's just picking a random x and y  coordinate within the square at with a  completely uniform distribution and  doing that 10,000 times you can see it  just kind of turns into a grayish box to  the left of that we're doing the same  thing except before generating a point  the the box is split into 16 sub boxes  each of those boxes is assigned a weight  and a box to generate a point in is  chosen based on that weighted  distribution before drawing it so what I  like about this is that I didn't like  you can tell that the the top-left boxes  are a little bit they're weighted higher  because they're darker and at the bottom  left they're weighted less the the  weight is less but kind of like an  interesting pattern fell out of this and  it was totally random like the little  Center there just looks like for like a  little window and then it looks like  it's kind of boxed by other things but  but it is 16 completely independent  squares so moving down into the bottom  on the right is a normal distribution  centered around the center of the square  both x and y coordinates are normally  distributed around the middle and then  on the left is kind of a combination of  the two so the x coordinate is is chosen  in a uniform distribution along the top  and then the Y coordinates are are  normally distributed  actually half normally distributed  because they fold over the top like that  along the top so the other really nice  kind of pseudo-random thing that we can  talk about is purling or simplex noise  this is a really really useful tool for  introducing movement into your  generative art the idea behind noise is  that you have or at least these two  noise algorithms I don't know if this is  true in general the idea behind simplex  or Perlin noise is that you can give it  a vector of n of dimension N and it'll  spit out a number between 0 and 1 the  interesting property is that any inputs  that are close together if you have two  vectors in 2d space that are close  together if you call noise on those  there the noise value will be very close  in value as well so this is really cool  for for instance like making this cool  3d this cool animation so each frame you  can kind of tell well let me back up a  little bit so what's happening here is  I'm sampling a sampling Perlin noise  sampling simplex noise at a whole bunch  of grid points and then I'm taking  whatever value that is and turning it  into a unit vector at that spot that  points in an angle that corresponds to  the number between 0 and 1 so it's just  then zero and one is interpolated  between 0 and 2pi and then we use that  as an angle to kind of generate that  little arrow so at each slice of this  animation at each frame you can see kind  of a slice of 2 dimensional simplex  noise and you can you can see that  things there are around each other kind  of follow the same pattern and your eye  will kind of flow through this in a  smooth way and then additionally this is  actually three dimensional simplex noise  that I'm using here because I want the  transitions between frames to be nice as  well so you're kind of seeing this like  three dimensional object kind of sliced  through using this  this simplex implementation but this is  a very very common tool for introducing  movement into into generative art cool  so art wouldn't be generative art  without algorithms and so here I want to  talk a little bit about just a common  algorithm class and a couple of  different different algorithms in this  class of algorithms the this is a piece  that I did call acid city and the whole  thing revolves around like a Markov  chain based subdivision algorithm of  quadrilaterals and this is kind of a  simplified version of what I'm doing  there so imagine you have solved a  quadrilateral what's happening here is  at every I'm basically doing a recursive  algorithm where the quadrilateral splits  over and over and over again and the way  that it splits is it looks for either a  horizontal or a vertical slice to kind  of cut the thing in half and then it  uses a normally distributed variable to  kind of shift around that slice and if  you pay like really close attention  you'll see that on each successive  iteration of this it looks like it kind  of alternates between vertical and  horizontal slicing and that's just an  implementation detail where like I'm I'm  taking the the previous state and kind  of trying to do the opposite most of the  time but this is a really effective a  really effective algorithm it's kind of  visually appealing just in this basic  state and it's not very hard to do so it  makes it very it makes it a very like  attractive thing to start trying so this  is subdivision of quadrilaterals cool so  the next thing I want to talk about is  chikan curve subdivision this is a like  a modified subdivision algorithm and  what I mean by that is the idea is to  subdivide line segments like we talked  about earlier but the way that the the  line segments are subdivided is by  taking  a line segment and splitting it into  three parts the part between 0 and 25  percent apart between 75% and a hundred  percent and then the middle part the 25%  to 75% but the key here is that you drop  the endpoints and only keep the middle  segment and what this ends up doing is  approximating a curve for a bunch of  control points it's a super super simple  algorithm but it's extremely effective  and like it'll take you ten minutes to  write but this is kind of what it  produces so this is kind of the same  same generation algorithm as the as the  path that we saw earlier but I'm running  in iterative chikan curve subdivision on  it and so you can kind of see it just  approximates this over time super clever  that's one of my favorite algorithms I  love it okay so we haven't talked about  color at all and the the main thing that  I want to say about color is that it's  extremely difficult it's much much much  harder than you might expect the it you  can use completely random colors and  that's probably not going to turn out  super well you can also use color  palettes that are generated by other  people on the internet that can work  I've done that in the past and it's  worked ok but in general it it's one of  those things that's really difficult to  wrap your head around but I do have one  piece of advice that I like to give to  people and it's to avoid RGB because RGB  is really difficult for us to think  about like if if you wanted me to  generate like a pastel yellow and in RGB  coordinates I would have absolutely no  idea how to do that  so what I like to use is HSV HSV is a  color space that stands for hue  saturation and value and the idea is  that I mean you have three three  different components of this and you can  tweak them in in whatever way you want  but it's a lot easier to talk about hues  saturations and values so hue is some  number between 0 and 360 which  represents the degree value of your of  your hue  it starts at red and you know goes  through the rainbow and loops back  around to read the saturation is how how  like vibrant your color is like the  vibrancy of your color and a lot of in  in like the real world we don't have a  lot of super saturated colors so it's  nice to stick around kind of the middle  component of this and that's just some  floating-point value between zero and  one and then value is a number another  number between zero and one which  represents that lightness the the value  that we talked about when we were  talking about elements so it's nice to  have that kind of correspondence if you  ask me to generate like a pastel yellow  here and HSV I would say you know maybe  maybe like 40 degrees probably not very  saturated because it's a pastel color  and then the value of that will probably  be kind of high so a nice bright pastel  yellow it's much easier to talk about  HSV and I would recommend using it even  though a lot of times it's not the  default when you're working with  graphics libraries I will mention that  this piece right here uses a technique  that I called color bza too  it's beyond the scope of this talk but  I'd love to talk about it with anybody  afterwards if you're curious and I've  also done a couple of experiments with  like genetic algorithms trying to pit  color schemes against one another  and kind of evolved a good color scheme  that I like and that's kind of in early  stages I just I haven't gotten too far  with it yet but it's somewhat promising  so the next thing I want to talk about  here is texture this is another thing  that computers are good at but they  don't tell you they're good at it  so it's really compelling to draw a  square and just fill it because that's  really easy to do but we can introduce  texture into our generative art I was  inspired to do this experiment by this  guy Casimir Malevich came up with this  idea that he really wanted to paint a  black square back in 1915 and he just  became obsessed with the idea and ended  up you know doing this and now it's a  famous work of art but what I think is  kind of interesting about that is you  know he was probably thinking about  doing something so perfect and back in  1915 now that was really hard to do but  now we have computers and we can  basically replicate this process in two  seconds you know draw a square on a  screen and fill it with black so I  wanted to kind of flip that idea on its  head and in draw like a black square  that had texture involved like the  texture that he might have gotten from  the paint instead of just doing the easy  thing and filling it with black so this  is what I came up with it's pretty  simple so this is an animation of three  black squares each increasing in value  or decreasing in value I guess but the  idea if you look closely I'm just  generating a whole bunch of square  circles they have various radii and  positions within the circle but you  stack enough of them and you get this  kind of interesting texture I think that  as it gets darker it kind of reminds me  of if you were to take like a ballpoint  pen and just like scribble for a long  time but it's kind of this interesting  emergent property  that's super easy to do but you just  have to sort of think about texture a  little bit more when you're doing  generative art like I could have drawn  the black square with with a fill but  thinking about texture in this way kind  of tends to produce more interesting  things and it's not really that hard  given that computers are fast so it's  kind of fun and I used that technique in  this tile piece as well cool so now I  want to talk about the principle of  emergence this animation is paused for a  second because I want to explain what's  going to happen before it does so  imagine a square you can imagine a  square you guys can handle that now take  the midpoints of each edge of that  Square and connect them like you get a  little diamond shape and if you keep  doing that then you twist back into a  square and you twist into a diamond and  each respect and everything gets smaller  and you kind of converge on this cool  thing that's what this piece does but  what I'm doing is instead of using  squares I'm generating shapes from  randomly generated points at the top and  bottom of the image so there are a whole  bunch of points that are invisibly  generated at the top on the bottom  they're connected randomly and I run  this algorithm on the bunch of times to  and and then draw each successive  iteration and then they're clipped  inside these to offset rectangles so but  you'll see that the result is sort of  surprising it surprised me at least  yeah I think that's like a pretty neat  texture was something that I was  definitely not expecting but I was just  kind of messing around with the the  chikan curve algorithm actually and came  upon this because if you shrink the line  segment down to one point in the center  that's exactly what you get what I just  described instead of using the  traditional chikan curve algorithm to  shrink the the line segment a little you  shrink it all the way down to the center  of the of the point and yeah this was  one of the first pieces that I ever  printed and sold so I have a soft spot  for it but I just really like the  animation I think it's kind of fun but  on the idea of the principle of  emergence this I didn't expect to happen  and it did happen and you can kind of  see that there's just there's this  structure here that you might not be  able to figure out if you were to look  at it without my explanation and that's  kind of what the principle of emergence  is all about cool so that's pretty much  all I've got it I I want to encourage  people to go try this stuff out on their  own so there are a bunch of different  libraries that will do this some of them  that are very commonly used are like  processing and it's JavaScript  counterpart p5.js I personally is the  Cairo graphics toolkit I use bindings  from Haskell to talk to the C library  pyro  open frameworks is kind of this  collection of things a lot of  audio-visual designers will use it and  do generative work with that so find the  library start messing with it and find a  community the community around  generative art is often just called  generative we are in a lot of places we  were on Twitter Instagram there's a  really great subreddit if you're into  that but you know the hashtag generative  will get you a community of people we're  friendly and we like to help so if  you're at all interested in this kind of  thing I encourage you to give it a shot  and yeah make art  [Applause]  I do have a couple of questions if you  all are interested the first question  here is how do you pick the best  iteration of the art when you're  generating it that is really hard I the  the piece of this that I didn't talk  about is that half of generative art I  think is curation a whole lot of  figuring out which things to publish and  show it's just a giant component of it  and for me it's a lot of times it's just  hard like I've gotten better at it over  time and I don't really know how to  answer the question but eventually I 